  "notes": "An ichidan verb meaning to fasten pages or documents together. A common office and school vocabulary word. Not to be confused with the homophone {閉|と}じる (to close, to shut).\n\nWRITING:\nThe kanji {綴|つづり} (spelling, binding) has the {糸|いと} radical, reflecting the original meaning of sewing or stitching together.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {書類|しょるい}を{綴|と}じる — to bind documents\n- ファイルに{綴|と}じる — to file in a binder\n- ホチキスで{綴|と}じる — to staple together\n- {糸|いと}で{綴|と}じる — to sew together\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {閉|と}じる — to close, to shut; a homophone with a completely different meaning (closing a door, a book, one's eyes, etc.)\n- {束|たば}ねる — to bundle together; used for grouping items into a bundle, while {綴|と}じる specifically implies fastening flat items like paper",
